1. Scenario & Geometry Mismatch: In `twin_v2`, the robot drives across a flat platform and drops off a 0.26 m ledge at t = 2.25 s. Because it approaches the step at 38.0° and a high speed of 1.03 m/s, the front-right wheel drops at 00:02.250 and the front-left wheel follows ~0.15 s later (00:02.375–00:02.500), causing the chassis to pitch nose-down off the ledge (`nose_over`, reaching 81.7° tilt at t = 3.15 s) before rolling onto its right side. In the real clip, there is no curb: the robot starts on indoor carpet facing a standing dog ~0.8 m ahead, drives forward (t = 0.52–2.40 s, peak ~0.8 m/s) passing just to the right of the dog's legs to stop under a desk, reverses back out (t = 3.60–4.60 s) to ~0.25 m directly behind the dog's hind legs, and then accelerates forward again at t = 6.56 s (ay_g = 0.432 g, reaching ~0.45 m/s at 0° approach angle) straight into the dog's hind legs at t = 6.86–7.06 s (00:07.375–00:08.250 in video, which lags the IMU by ~1.3–1.4 s).

2. Pitch/Roll Kinematics Mismatch: Whereas the sim twin pitches forward (`nose_over`) off a curb at t = 2.25–3.15 s, the real robot is pushed/lifted backward by the dog collision at t = 7.06–7.16 s (ay_g spikes to +1.426 g), pitching backward ~85° onto its rear bumper (ay_g ≈ +0.90 to +0.98 g, az_g ≈ 0.0 to 0.25 g from t = 7.16 to 9.56 s while the front camera points straight up at the ceiling fan and the underside of the dog for 2.6 s). Only at t = 9.66–9.96 s (00:11.125–00:11.500 in video) does the robot topple laterally from its rear onto its right side (`rollover`, settling at ax_g ≈ +1.0 g, ay_g ≈ 0.0 g, az_g ≈ -0.1 g from t = 9.96 s onward).

3. Simulator Note: To represent the true event, the scene must be a flat indoor `pedestrian` (animal) interaction at ~0.45 m/s head-on (`0°`) into the dog's hindquarters (radius ~0.15 m). (If approximating a pure right-side `rollover` without `nose_over` using a curb-drop proxy in a simulator lacking upward/backward animal forces, the curb drop would require a much shallower oblique angle of ~55°–65°, slower speed of ~0.45 m/s, and lower step height of ~0.18 m so the right-side wheels drop while the left-side wheels stay supported.)
